            <ArticleTitle>Symptom check: is it GERD?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>103-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Despite the vast number of individuals living with gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), establishing a diagnosis can be difficult. Detailed patient history and symptom review are critical. Nurses can play an integral role in gathering information that may lead to a definitive diagnosis. In addition to the classic symptoms of GERD-acid reflux and heartburn-several atypical conditions may also be present. Understanding the diverse and subtle nature of presenting symptoms can assist in establishing a GERD diagnosis. This column examines the atypical presentations of GERD, including laryngopharyngeal symptoms, chronic cough, asthma, and dental erosions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
